{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' docs/agents/roles/architect.md; rg -n '\"'^#{1,4} |non-negotiable|No hidden|No implicit|No raw|No shared|No layer|No assumed|cab33f84|rebuild|backward|legacy|constructor metadata|supported values'\"' docs/design/datom.world.md docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md docs/design/dao.jing.md docs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md\"","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Lead System Architect role definition for datom.world\n---\n\n# ROLE: Lead System Architect\n\n## Domain Scope\n\n- Foundational axioms, architectural invariants, and design contracts\n- Subsystem boundaries, interpreters, host isolation, and explicit causality\n- Cross-platform architecture, migration strategy, and compatibility boundaries\n- ADR coherence, domain vocabulary, and theoretical grounding where useful\n- Architecture review and sign-off for high-risk or cross-cutting changes\n\nThis role owns no permanent file list. Each task defines the artifacts it may\ninspect or change and any permitted expansion.\n\n## Core Responsibilities\n\n1. **Protect Invariants**: Strictly enforce the 6 non-negotiable invariants (no hidden global state, no implicit control flow, no raw callbacks, no shared mutable state, no layer collapsing, no assumed graphs).\n2. **Decompose Complexity**: When new requirements emerge, decompose them into append-only streams, explicit interpreters, and immutable datom representations.\n3. **Malleability Governance**: Ensure architecture adheres to bounded complexity, loose coupling, and high cohesion as defined in [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../malleability.md).\n4. **Lineage Alignment**: Keep designs grounded in Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp architectural lineage.\n\n## Delegation Prompt Template\n\n```text\nCreated-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\nCreated-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\n\n# Task: <Task Name>\n\nRole: Lead System Architect\n\nImplementers:\n- Model: <model-name> | Assigned: <local timestamp> | Status: active | Rationale: <why>\n\nPerform a read-only architecture review of <design-change>.\n\nRead first:\n- docs/design/datom.world.md\n- <target-design-file>\n- <relevant-implementation-boundary>\n- <relevant-contract-tests>\n\nEvaluate foundational invariants, ownership boundaries, explicit state and\ncontrol flow, concurrency and linearization, dynamic extension, host isolation,\nCLJ/CLJS/CLJD portability, migration risk, completion criteria, and design\ncontradictions. Distinguish architectural defects from implementation gaps or\nintentionally deferred work. Do not edit files.\n\nBegin the final response exactly with:\nCompleted-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\nCompleted-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\n\nThen report: severity | file:line | invariant/evidence | recommended correction.\nAlso confirm the requested properties that passed review.\n```\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:5:# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:7:## Core Philosophy\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:14:### Foundational Axioms\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:21:## Non-Negotiable Invariants\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:30:## Tuples and Datoms\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:38:## Streams\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:64:### Host Boundaries\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:122:## Architectural Commitments\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:129:## Design Principles\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:147:## Code Style\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:155:## Development Workflow\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:163:### Problem Decomposition\ndocs/design/datom.world.md:172:## Operational & Specialized Guides\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:1:# Multihash-Style Content Addressing for DaoJing and yin.vm\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:5:## Decision\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:20:There is no migration or backward-compatibility project. Datom.world has no deployed DaoJing stores, published manifests, externally held addresses, or legacy systems. The registry and BLAKE3 default therefore land as one clean contract: fixtures and development stores are regenerated rather than migrated.\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:24:## Address format\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:45:## Invariants\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:63:## DaoJing design\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:65:### Closed algorithm registry\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:98:### Public digest primitives\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:120:### `canonical-bytes`\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:139:### `content-hash`\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:168:### `segment-key`\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:193:### Address parsing\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:227:### `segment-matches?`\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:250:### `materialize!`\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:283:### `get`\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:295:### File-backend codec round-trip\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:308:## Clean introduction\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:324:## Call-site audit and required dispositions\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:335:### Direct `jing/sha256` consumers\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:351:Neither disposition is a legacy-support exception. They are independent contract boundaries that happen to specify SHA-256.\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:355:### Primitive-profile identifier\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:375:### DaoSpace schema identity\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:393:### Address-directed validation sites\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:421:### Address-directed mint sites\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:443:### Documentation-only SHA assumptions\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:449:## yin.vm H and R disposition\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:477:## Host libraries\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:491:### Maintenance risks\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:508:## Sequencing with canonical CBOR\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:547:## Phased rollout\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:549:### H0: contract and evidence\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:570:### H1: registry and whole-system cutover\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:598:### H2: multi-algorithm verification and hardening\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:625:## Test obligations\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:653:## Explicit non-goals\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:1:# DaoJing: The Content-Addressed Storage Observer\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:30:## Definition\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:75:## The intake pool\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:109:## Publication from an agent\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:148:## Materialization rule\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:179:### Canonical encoding\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:181:Content addressing is meaningful only when equal supported values produce the\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:216:## Multihash Content Addressing & Algorithm Registry\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:254:### Host Dependencies and Licenses\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:264:### Maintenance Obligations: Call-Site Classification & AST Lint Guard\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:288:## Storage ignorance\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:305:## Physical intake versus semantic composition\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:319:## Reads\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:341:## Cursor tracking and recovery\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:372:## Resource lifecycle\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:383:## Implemented surface\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:417:  rebuild the in-memory map of address to bytes. Replay parses every\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:510:## Open items and current limitations\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:527:  Dart producer must clear constructor metadata itself\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:595:## Lineage\ndocs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:1:# DaoJing: Backend-Independent CBOR Storage\ndocs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:13:## Objective and invariant\ndocs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:59:## Community pre