ic x, the same distinctions content\\n   847\\t   addressing makes: kind, then within float64 the exact bits (so the zero\\n   848\\t   sign counts, and every NaN is the one canonical NaN), within decimal the\\n   849\\t   exponent (scale) and mantissa, within rational the reduced numerator and\\n   850\\t   denominator. Integer width is not identity. Built from strings and\\n   851\\t   keywords, so equal content gives equal keys on every host.\\\"\\n   852\\t  [x]\\n   853\\t  (case (numeric-kind x)\\n   854\\t    :integer (do #?(:cljs (when (and (number? x)\\n   855\\t                                     (not (js/Number.isSafeInteger x)))\\n   856\\t                            (refuse :unsupported-value\\n   857\\t                                    (str \\\"unsafe integral JavaScript number: \\\"\\n   858\\t                                         \\\"its value is already rounded\\\"))))\\n   859\\t                 [::integer (str (big x))])\\n   860\\t    :float64 (let [[neg e f] (float-fields (float-value x))]\\n   861\\t               (if (and (= e 0x7ff) (pos? (bsign f)))\\n   862\\t                 [::float64 :nan]\\n   863\\t                 [::float64 neg e (str f)]))\\n   864\\t    :decimal [::decimal\\n   865\\t              (str (decimal-exponent x))\\n   866\\t              (str (big (decimal-mantissa x)))]\\n   867\\t    :rational (let [[_ n d] (exact x)]\\n   868\\t                [::rational (str n) (str d)])))\\n   869\\t\\n   870\\t\\n   871\\t(defn content-key\\n   872\\t  \\\"A host value whose host `=` and `hash` agree with content=: numbers by\\n   873\\t   their kind-strict identity, byte strings by content, identifiers by their\\n   874\\t   namespace and name fields, lists and vectors alike (README ruling A6)\\n   875\\t   element by element, maps and sets by members, metadata ignored, any other\\n   876\\t   value as itself. A caller keys a host map or set by it to get\\n   877\\t   kind-strict content identity from host collections (JVM `=` merges\\n   878\\t   0.0 with -0.0 and 1.0M with 1.00M; ClojureScript merges slash-crossed\\n   879\\t   identifiers). The same traversal as equiv, with the kind-strict numeric\\n   880\\t   key in place of num=.\\\"\\n   881\\t  [x]\\n   882\\t  (cond\\n   883\\t    (numeric? x) (numeric-content-key x)\\n   884\\t    (wire/byte-payload? x) [::bytes (bytes-hex x)]\\n   885\\t    (or (keyword? x) (symbol? x)) [::identifier (identifier-key x)]\\n   886\\t    (sequential-value? x) (mapv content-key x)\\n   887\\t    (map? x) (into {} (map (fn [[k v]] [(content-key k) (content-key v)])) x)\\n   888\\t    (set? x) (into #{} (map content-key) x)\\n   889\\t    :else x))\\n   890\\t\\n   891\\t\\n   892\\t(defn content=\\n   893\\t  \\\"Portable kind-strict decoded equality: two values are content= exactly\\n   894\\t   when content addressing would give them the same identity. Numbers must\\n   895\\t   share kind (integer, float64, decimal, rational), and within kind the\\n   896\\t   float zero sign, the decimal scale and the exact value; host integer\\n   897\\t   width is not significant, and canonical NaNs are equal (every NaN has\\n   898\\t   the one canonical encoding). Collections recurse the way equiv does,\\n   899\\t   metadata ignored. Unlike equiv, (content= 1 1.0) is false.\\\"\\n   900\\t  [a b]\\n   901\\t  (or (identical? a b)\\n   902\\t      (= (content-key a) (content-key b))))\\n   903\\t\\n   904\\t\\n   905\\t(defn content-hash\\n  1010\\t(defn- node-hash\\n  1011\\t  \\\"§5: hash(node) = SHA-256(dimension-hash || encode(tag-specific-slots-\\n  1012\\t   in-descriptor-order)). The preimage is the dimension hash's hex\\n  1013\\t   followed by the framed slots under the settled byte rules; the shape —\\n  1014\\t   descriptor order, tags, byte-count length delimiters, ordered child\\n  1015\\t   hashes — is what D2 fixed and D3 settled. Markers are never among the\\n  1016\\t   encodings: only present, non-marker slots reach here.\\\"\\n  1017\\t  [encodings]\\n  1018\\t  (jing/sha256 (apply str dimension-hash encodings)))\\n  1019\\t\\n  1020\\t\\n  1021\\t;; =============================================================================\\n  1022\\t;; D2: hash-consing and the projected records (§4)\\n  1023\\t;; =============================================================================\\n  1024\\t\\n  1025\\t(defn- slot-encodings\\n  1026\\t  \\\"The descriptor-order encodings of one record's present, non-marker\\n  1027\\t   slots — with the dimension hash, the only input to a node hash.\\n  1028\\t   Children already carry child hashes, so this is total over a record\\n  1029\\t   and serves both the mint and the storage reader's content check.\\\"\\n  1030\\t  [record]\\n  1031\\t  (for [[slot type role] dimension-slots\\n  1032\\t        :when (and (not= :marker role) (contains? record slot))]\\n  1033\\t    (encode-typed type (get record slot))))\\n  1034\\t\\n  1035\\t\\n  1036\\t(defn- mint-record\\n  1037\\t  \\\"Hash-cons one resolved node (§4-§5) whose children are already\\n  1038\\t   minted, their hashes in `child-hashes` ({slot h}, an ordered vector\\n  1039\\t   under :yin.debruijn/operands): the node hash over the dimension hash\\n  1040\\t   and the node's tag-specific slots in descriptor order, then the record\\n  1041\\t   — the node with child slots carrying child hashes, scalars in their\\n  1042\\t   canonical spellings, and :yin.debruijn/hash added. Returns [h acc']\\n  1043\\t   over {:hashcons preimage->h, :records h->record, :mints n}; :mints\\n  1044\\t   counts every call, the walk's instrumentation seam. The consing memo\\n  1045\\t   is keyed on the node's preimage — the concatenated slot encodings —\\n  1046\\t   never on Clojure =, which merges a list literal with a vector literal\\n  1047\\t   and the signed zeros; a preimage hit implies the identical hash and,\\n  1048\\t   records carrying canonical spellings, the identical record. The memo\\n  1049\\t   stays an optimisation only: identity is the hash either way, so\\n  1050\\t   traversal order never enters it.\\\"\\n  1051\\t  [node child-hashes acc]\\n  1052\\t  (let [acc (update acc :mints inc)\\n  1053\\t        record (reduce (fn [r [slot _type role]]\\n  1054\\t                         (cond\\n  1055\\t                           (or (= :marker role) (not (contains? node slot))) r\\n  1056\\t                           (= :child role) (assoc r slot (get child-hashes slot))\\n  1057\\t                           ;; the carried D3 obligation: a record's stored\\n  1058\\t                           ;; scalar is its canonical spelling, so equal\\n  1059\\t                           ;; fingerprints imply equal records\\n  1060\\t                           :else (assoc r slot (canonical-value (get node slot)))))\\n  1061\\t                       node\\n  1062\\t                       dimension-slots)\\n  1063\\t        encodings (slot-encodings record)\\n  1064\\t        preimage (apply str encodings)]\\n  1065\\t    (if-let [cached (find (:hashcons acc) preimage)]\\n  1066\\t      [(val cached) acc]\\n  1067\\t      (let [h (node-hash encodings)]\\n  1068\\t        [h (-> acc\\n  1069\\t               (assoc-in [:hashcons preimage] h)\\n  1070\\t               (assoc-in [:records h] (assoc record :yin.debruijn/hash h)))]))))\\n  1071\\t\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:65:(def canonical-value-table\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:145:     :values canonical-value-table}]\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:302:(defn canonical-value?\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:529:    :canonical (canonical-value? v)\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:807:        (:classes canonical-value-table)))\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:964:(defn canonical-value\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:994:                                        [(canonical-value k) (canonical-value x)]))\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1000:      :set (let [canonical (into (empty v) (map canonical-value) v)]\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1005:      :vector (mapv canonical-value v)\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1006:      :list (apply list (map canonical-value v))\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1060:                           :else (assoc r slot (canonical-value (get node slot)))))\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1185:    (= :canonical type) (canonical-value? v)\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1237:   `canonical-value` gives every stored scalar. Exact, never =: Dart's\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1296:                          {:rule :noncanonical-value,\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1313:   in its canonical spelling (:noncanonical-value) — because the\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:834:(defn- strip-reader-positions\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:845:                                       [(strip-reader-positions k)\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:846:                                        (strip-reader-positions v)]))\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:848:                 (set? x) (into (empty x) (map strip-reader-positions) x)\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:851:                 (vector? x) (mapv strip-reader-positions x)\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:855:                                   (apply list (map strip-reader-positions x))\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:862:      (let [m' (strip-reader-positions\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:929:             (:data :key) (strip-reader-positions v)\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/1790274832538-architect-cbor-dart-diagnosis.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:60:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_30\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-24 18:39:39 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 01:39:39 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\\n\\nThe failures share one Dart-specific source: `cljd.core/list` produces lists carrying `{:tag PersistentList}` where `PersistentList` is a Dart `Type` object. The pinned core source seeds `list` from a type-hinted empty list ([core.cljd:3431](</Users/sto/.gitlibs/libs/tensegritics/clojuredart/81b5c03a55cf52b21dc0be8ccfa4827b9889f488/clj/src/cljd/core.cljd:3431>)); its generated Dart attaches that exact tag, and `-conj` preserves it ([core.dart:31348](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/lib/cljd-out/cljd/core.dart:31348), [core.dart:15929](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/lib/cljd-out/cljd/core.dart:15929)). Jing strips reader-position keys but retains `:tag`, then correctly refuses the unsupported `Type` ([cbor.cljc:1074](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:1074), [cbor.cljc:1125](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:1125)).\\n\\n1. The Jing hash test constructs both an inner and outer list with `list` ([jing_test.cljc:225](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing_test.cljc:225)). Their constructor metadata—not the set encoding—causes the refusal recorded at [test-cljd-verify.log:2167](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/target/test-cljd-verify.log:2167).\\n2. The VM test likewise supplies a runtime `(list 1 2)` in its collection case ([vm_test.cljc:625](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm_test.cljc:625)). `strip-reader-positions` clears metadata on the list it constructs, but then restores the input list’s retained `:tag` ([vm.cljc:852](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:852), [vm.cljc:858](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:858)). The resulting address calculation refuses that `Type` ([test-cljd-verify.log:431](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/target/test-cljd-verify.log:431)).\\n3. The pipeline test supplies a runtime list ([pipeline_test.cljc:584](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc:584)), but this case also has a production defect: `debruijn/canonical-value` builds a *new* list with `apply list` and does not clear its constructor metadata ([debruijn.cljc:1006](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1006)). That list enters a projected record ([debruijn.cljc:1060](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1060)) and the Jing envelope ([pipeline.cljc:42](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/pipeline.cljc:42)). The store catches encoding refusal as `:diagnostic` ([pipeline.cljc:111](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/pipeline.cljc:111)); the log reports only that outcome, not its diagnostic rule ([test-cljd-verify.log:1064](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/target/test-cljd-verify.log:1064)).\\n\\nPrescribed local fix: clear metadata at the construction sites, not in the CBOR codec. At [debruijn.cljc:1006](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1006), return `(with-meta (apply list (map canonical-value v)) nil)`; this projection already excludes metadata from its canonical value identity. In [jing_test.cljc:225](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing_test.cljc:225) and [vm_test.cljc:626](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm_test.cljc:626), explicitly clear metadata on runtime-list fixtures while preserving their list shape. Keep the pipeline test’s ordinary input list as a regression test for the projection fix. Do **not** globally discard `:tag`: the design requires preserving arbitrary user metadata and explicitly forbids that workaround ([dao.jing.cbor.md:179](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:179)).\\n\\nThis evidence qualifies as a pinned ClojureDart constructor/compiler bug, but a dependency bump is not a demonstrated fix: a locally cached newer June 2026 checkout retains the same `list` source ([core.cljd:3436](</Users/sto/.gitlibs/libs/tensegritics/clojuredart/178e4fb0286eddb665f3cdd0850919961ec436d5/clj/src/cljd/core.cljd:3436>)). I could not verify the current upstream HEAD. A trial bump is reasonable only after checking that it removes the generated `:tag`, followed by all three host lanes; it should not replace the local projection fix without that evidence.\\n\\nThe design’s unqualified supported-values claim for lists and its assumption that no current producer emits an unsupported value are too broad for Dart ([dao.jing.cbor.md:147](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:147)). Its later constructor-metadata warning correctly anticipates the problem ([dao.jing.cbor.md:179](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:179)), but must explicitly cover Jing-bound lists reconstructed by internal producers such as the de Bruijn projection, not just caller-created lists.\\n\\nThis was static diagnosis only; I ran no test suite or build and edited no files.\\n\\nStatus: DIAGNOSIS COMPLETE\"}}\n?? collab/1790255038476-reviewer-universal-linker-consensus-r6.prompt.md\n?? collab/1790255306752-architect-universal-linker-reconciliation-r7.glm-5.3.stdout.log\n?? collab/1790255306752-architect-universal-linker-reconciliation-r7.prompt.md\n?? collab/1790255808829-reviewer-universal-linker-consensus-r7.gpt-6-sol.findings.md\n?? collab/1790255808829-reviewer-universal-linker-consensus-r7.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1790255808829-reviewer-universal-linker-consensus-r7.prompt.md\n?? collab/1790256032740-architect-universal-linker-reconciliation-r8.glm-5.3.stdout.log\n?? collab/1790256032740-architect-universal-linker-reconciliation-r8.prompt.md\n?? collab/1790256330629-reviewer-universal-linker-consensus-r8.gpt-6-sol.findings.md\n?? collab/1790256330629-reviewer-universal-linker-consensus-r8.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1790256330629-reviewer-universal-linker-consensus-r8.prompt.md\n?? collab/1790256558688-architect-universal-linker-reconciliation-r9.glm-5.3.stdout.log\n?? collab/1790256558688-architect-universal-linker-reconciliation-r9.prompt.md\n?? collab/1790256720621-reviewer-universal-linker-consensus-r9.gpt-6-sol.findings.md\n?? collab/1790256720621-reviewer-universal-linker-consensus-r9.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1790256720621-reviewer-universal-linker-consensus-r9.prompt.md\n?? collab/1790256883015-architect-universal-linker-reconciliation-r10.glm-5.3.stdout.log\n?? collab/1790256883015-architect-universal-linker-reconciliation-r10.prompt.m