{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"for p in collab/1790245519256-reviewer-daojing-cbor-swap.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md collab/1790264589986-reviewer-daojing-cbor-swap-fixes-r2.gpt-6-sol.findings.md collab/1790267002049-reviewer-daojing-cbor-swap-fixes-r3.glm-flash.findings.md collab/1790271252483-reviewer-daojing-cbor-swap-fixes-r4.gpt-6-sol.findings.md collab/1790279787648-reviewer-daojing-cbor-swap-fixes-r5.gpt-6-sol.findings.md collab/1790279991314-reviewer-daojing-cbor-swap-confirm-r6.gpt-6-sol.findings.md; do sed -n '1,100p' \\\"\"'$p\"; done'","aggregated_output":"I’m using the `code-review-pr` skill because this is a branch-level adversarial review. I’ll first load its review protocol and the four governing design documents, then inspect the branch diff and run only read-only checks/tests that do not modify repository state.\nThe governing contract is clear on the key failure modes: one CBOR encoder, strict ingress canonicality, byte-snapshot immutability, and nondestructive file replay before torn-tail truncation. I’m now establishing the exact branch delta and will trace those requirements through implementation and tests rather than relying on the task’s file list alone.\nThe core swap is indeed direct (`canonical-bytes` delegates only to `cbor/encode`), and memory/file now retain encoded byte snapshots. I’m checking the less obvious edges next: replay’s algorithm reconstruction, canonical-frame rejection before truncation, mutation isolation on returned binary values, and whether the updated tests actually exercise these paths.\nI found a concrete immutability breach: the memory handle now exposes its atom containing mutable host byte arrays, so callers can mutate stored addressed content behind the API. I’m doing a final static verification pass now, including the explicit ASCII/80-column gate and checking whether the tests cover the new byte helpers and replay format.\nThe focused JVM suite passes (80 tests, 3,200 assertions), and the changed implementation/test sources pass clj-kondo and cljstyle. Those checks do not cover the exposed-byte mutation path or enforce the requested ASCII/80-column limits; both remain review findings. I’m consolidating the final evidence now, including replay’s exact algorithm-selection behavior.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-24 10:33:59 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-24 17:33:59 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\nVerdict: request changes.\n\nP1 | [src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:104](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:104) | The public handle exposes `:state`, whose content now contains mutable host byte arrays. A reproduction mutated stored `[1 2]` into `[1 3]`; `mem/entries` returned the forged value while `jing/get` detected an integrity error. `entries` also decodes without address verification at line 118. | Keep the atom inaccessible, expose a closure-based snapshot view, and use `segment-value` for verified decoding. Add mutation-isolation tests for both input and exposed state/output.\n\nP1 | [src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:457](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:457) | The required shared byte-store boundary was not implemented. `segment-key` encodes once, then `materialize!` passes the original value to the backend; memory and file encode it again at [mem.cljc:38](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:38) and [file.cljc:344](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:344). Instrumentation confirmed two encoder calls for one insertion, contrary to the encode-once contract at [dao.jing.cbor.md:98](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:98). | Encode once in the Jing wrapper, derive the address from those bytes, and delegate through `:put-bytes-fn`/`:get-bytes-fn`; keep public APIs value-facing.\n\nP2 | [docs/design/dao.jing.md:190](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.md:190) | The authoritative design still declares the order-normalized printer current, lists it as an open item at line 486, and describes file records as `[address payload]` at line 407. This contradicts the implemented clean break. | Replace the transitional-printer sections with the canonical-CBOR and byte-store contracts, document `[digest payload-bytes]`, and retire the superseded open items.\n\nP2 | [test/dao/jing/file_test.cljc:222](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing/file_test.cljc:222) | The file fail-closed tests still inject EDN text at lines 229–235. They prove that arbitrary non-CBOR fails, but do not exercise valid-CBOR wrong shapes, bad digest lengths, digest mismatches, noncanonical payload bytes, or mutation isolation. | Construct raw CBOR frames for every replay refusal class and add binary/metadata/rich-number and byte-mutation round trips.\n\nP3 | [test/dao/data/psset_fixtures.cljc:1](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/data/psset_fixtures.cljc:1) | The strict hygiene gate fails: this generated file contains non-ASCII and 860 lines over 80 columns; representative long output starts at line 12. Other named files also contain non-ASCII or long lines, including [hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:94](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:94) and [digest-table.edn:104](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/resources/dao/jing/digest-table.edn:104). | Update the fixture generator to emit ASCII and wrap address literals, regenerate, and reflow the remaining named files.\n\nConfirmed correct:\n\n- `canonical-bytes` delegates directly to `cbor/encode`; the three printer functions are absent from production sources.\n- `segment-matches?` is total, while `segment-bytes` and `segment-value` perform the expected hash/codec validation.\n- File frames are canonical CBOR byte-string pairs. Replay infers the algorithm by testing the digest against each registered algorithm, reconstructs the address from the first match, rejects no-match frames, validates all complete frames, and only then truncates a torn tail: [file.cljc:116](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:116), [file.cljc:279](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:279).\n- No legacy decoder, printer alias, or old-address fallback was found.\n\nVerification: focused JVM suite passed 80 tests / 3,200 assertions; clj-kondo reported 0 errors and 0 warnings; cljstyle passed. No files were edited.\nI’m using the `code-review-pr` workflow for this follow-up. I’ll reread the four governing documents in their current state, then verify each prior finding against the fix delta and inspect the Node failure evidence. This will remain read-only.\nThe core fixes are present: the backends now retain byte snapshots, and `materialize!` hashes the bytes it passes to storage. I’m checking the remaining edge cases—especially whether validation happens before the snapshot is taken—and tracing the two Node failures back to their consumers.\nThe Node log shows two distinct symptoms of the same boundary mismatch: decoded `float64` carriers are rejected by a VM slot check, and a test compares them to bare JavaScript numbers. The codec document allows the carrier representation, but the current consumer contract and test do not yet accommodate it; I’m treating that as unresolved, not an expected green result.\nThe replay refusal tests now cover canonical CBOR wrong shapes, digest errors, noncanonical payloads, and nondestructive rejection; that prior gap is closed. I also found a stale registry design section that still calls the printer current, plus a snapshot-order race in both byte-store puts. I’m doing the final hygiene and contract cross-check before assigning severities.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-24 15:55:32 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-24 22:55:32 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\nThe five prior findings are now:\n\n1. **CLOSED** — The memory handle exposes closures rather than `:state`, returns byte copies, and verifies `entries` before decoding ([mem.cljc:96](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:96), [mem.cljc:132](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:132), [mem.cljc:145](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:145)). A separate concurrent-mutation issue remains below.\n2. **CLOSED** — `materialize!` encodes the payload once, hashes those bytes, and passes them to `:put-bytes-fn` ([jing.cljc:512](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:512)).\n3. **CLOSED** — `dao.jing.md` now specifies CBOR, byte stores, and `[digest payload-bytes]` frames ([dao.jing.md:193](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.md:193), [dao.jing.md:411](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.md:411)). The separate hash-registry design remains stale.\n4. **PARTIALLY CLOSED** — Replay tests now cover the requested CBOR refusal classes and unchanged-file rejection ([file_test.cljc:278](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing/file_test.cljc:278)). Memory tests cover caller-array isolation ([mem_test.cljc:234](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing/mem_test.cljc:234)); the file tests cover disk-byte corruption, but not caller input/output array mutation ([file_test.cljc:326](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing/file_test.cljc:326)).\n5. **CLOSED for the three previously named fixtures** — The current `psset_fixtures.cljc`, `hash_registry_contract_test.cljc`, and `digest-table.edn` contain only ASCII and lines of at most 80 columns. The wider changed-file hygiene gate still fails.\n\nActionable findings:\n\nP1 | [src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1176](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1176) | The Node run’s durable round-trip errors when `:yin.debruijn/value` contains decoded `#dao.jing/float64 1.5`; the canonical classifier accepts numbers but not this carrier ([debruijn.cljc:273](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:273), [test-cljs.log:311](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/target/test-cljs.log:311)). Its second failure compares a decoded carrier to bare `2.5` ([pipeline_test.cljc:585](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc:585), [test-cljs.log:316](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/target/test-cljs.log:316)). The codec *requires* a JS carrier on decode ([dao.jing.cbor.md:375](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:375)); these are unresolved consumer/test defects, not an accepted passing limitation. | Make the VM’s canonical-value and numeric paths carrier-aware, compare portable content in the test, and require a green Node suite.\n\nP2 | [src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:51](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:51) | Both byte-store puts verify a caller-owned mutable array *before* copying it ([file.cljc:348](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:348)). A concurrent caller mutation between validation and copy can store or append bytes that no longer hash to the address; duplicate checks also compare the original array ([mem.cljc:60](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:60), [file.cljc:360](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:360)). | Copy first, then validate, frame, and compare only the snapshot.\n\nP2 | [src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:1536](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:1536) | `decode` re-encodes every value. Consequently ordinary accepted-snapshot reads through `segment-value` re-encode too ([jing.cljc:470](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:470)), contrary to the explicit read-path contract ([dao.jing.cbor.md:124](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:124)). | Separate strict ingress canonicality validation from trusted-snapshot decoding while retaining address-hash verification on reads.\n\nP2 | [docs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:128](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:128) | This governing document still says `canonical-bytes` returns order-normalized print bytes and that CBOR is future work ([dao.jing.hash-registry.md:505](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:505)); the implementation directly calls `cbor/encode` ([jing.cljc:279](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:279)). | Update the registry document to describe the completed clean break and current digest inputs.\n\nP3 | [test/dao/jing/file_test.cljc:326](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing/file_test.cljc:326) | The new mutation test changes bytes on disk; it does not exercise mutation of the byte array passed to, or returned from, the file handle, unlike the memory tests ([mem_test.cljc:234](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing/mem_test.cljc:234)). | Add file-handle input and output array mutation-isolation tests, including a reopen check.\n\nP3 | [src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:1501](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:1501) | An added line is 82 columns. Across all 40 tracked changed files, the current-file scan finds non-ASCII in 22 files and over-80-column lines in 25; added-line examples also occur at [storage.cljc:3](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/data/btree/storage.cljc:3) and [remote/step.cljc:314](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/remote/step.cljc:314). Many wider-file violations predate these edits, but the requested global gate is not clean. | Reflow and ASCII-normalize the changed files, starting with added violations.\n\nReview was read-only. I relied on the retained Node log and static checks; I did not rerun the suites. The fresh JVM result supplied in the brief does not resolve the Node failures.\n\nVerdict: REQUEST CHANGES\n# Round-3 consensus verification — DaoJing CBOR swap (r2 delta)\n\nReviewer: ZCode subagent, GLM-5.3-Flash (independent of claude-sonnet-5 fix author).\nArtifacts: collab/1790267002049-reviewer-daojing-cbor-swap-fixes-r3.glm-flash.{prompt.md,findings.md}\nCompleted: 2026-09-25 ~00:15 +07. All work read-only; every claim verified against the tree.\n\n## Rulings on the six r2 findings\n\n1. **VM carrier awareness — CLOSED.** `numeric-class` tests the carrier first on every host\n   (`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:227` `(cbor/float64? v) :double`, host dispatch at 230-235),\n   and `canonical-class` routes carriers at `debruijn.cljc:299`. Host parity holds: `float64?`\n   (`src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:460-468`) matches each host's decode output (JVM `Double` at\n   cbor.cljc:525, CLJS `Float64` deftype decoded at cbor.cljc:1436-1440, Dart double);\n   decimal/rational carriers classify nil intentionally (outside the VM's §5 numeric domain);\n   forged native floats and tag 39 are refused at decode (cbor.cljc:1466, 1515), and a\n   non-carrier deftype/record falls through `canonical-class` to nil, so nothing smuggles\n   through as a plain number. `slot-kind-ok?`/`slot-shape-ok?` (`debruijn.cljc:537,1190`)\n   correctly reject carriers from `:int64` slots via the `number?` guard. pipeline_test now\n   compares portable content via `cbor/content=` at the three sites\n   (`test/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc:512-518, 559-562, 591-601`); these fail on pre-fix code.\n\n2. **Copy-before-validate — CLOSED.** `src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:53-54` copies first,\n   validates the snapshot, and the CAS duplicate comparison uses only the snapshot\n   (mem.cljc:62); `src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:348` copies first, validates (349-353), frames\n   (354) and compares (362) only the snapshot. No read of caller-owned `bs` remains after the\n   copy in either backend; TOCTOU is closed.\n\n3. **Trusted reads must not re-encode — PARTIALLY CLOSED.** The split is correct:\n   `decode-snapshot` (`cbor.cljc:1524-1538`) drops only the re-encode check; `decode`\n   (1541-1550) retains full strictness; `segment-value` hash-verifies then snapshot-decodes\n   (`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:463-471`), and all its consumers are trusted reads\n   (`jing.cljc:563`, `mem.cljc:147`, `file.cljc:449`, `dao/space/index.cljc:629,1181`). Strict\n   decode is retained at `remote.cljc:50`, `dht.cljc:174`, `dht/node.cljc:120`,\n   `file.cljc:124,145`, `debruijn_linker.cljc:199` — but the fix report's \"all ingress sites\n   still call strict decode\" is false: the remote client receipt silently regressed (P1 below).\n\n4. **Hash-registry doc — CLOSED.** `docs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:129-131` states\n   `canonical-bytes` delegates to `dao.jing.cbor/encode` and the print encoder \"is gone (clean\n   break, 2026-09-24)\"; :509-512 records the clean break as complete. `dao.jing.md:512-513`\n   marks the transitional printer retired.\n\n5. **File-handle isolation tests — CLOSED.** `test/dao/jing/file_test.cljc:228` mutates the\n   input array after put and checks the in-memory snapshot, `get-bytes`, a reopen, and\n   `records`; `:254` mutates the output array after get and checks later reads plus a reopen.\n   They exercise exactly the claimed paths.\n\n6. **Added-line hygiene — CLOSED for the five flagged lines**, with one new violation found\n   (P3 below). Every remaining violation in the touched files matches HEAD text exactly,\n   verified line-by-line.\n\n## Actionable findings\n\nP1 | src/cljc/dao/jing/remote/step.cljc:304 | The remote get-content client receipt decodes\nuntrusted reply bytes with `jing/segment-value` (`(jing/segment-value address bs)`), which\nsince the r2 delta is hash-verify + `cbor/decode-snapshot` (`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:471`) —\nno canonicality check. The site's own comment (step.cljc:295-297: \"decode as one canonical\npayload before a value is published\") and the contract (`docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:125-128`\n— canonicality verification at each ingress of bytes Jing did not encode itself,\n\"remote/DHT receipt ... before storage, caching, or exposure\"; :458-459; :638-640 \"Hash-valid\nnoncanonical payloads fail at ingress\") require strict decode here, as the storage-side\nreceipts still do (`remote.cljc:50`, `dht.cljc:174`, `dht/node.cljc:120`). A malicious\naddress+server pair can serve a hash-valid, profile-valid but noncanonical payload whose\ncanonical bytes hash to a different address; it is published as `:found? true :value decoded`\n(step.cljc:316) and silently breaks content-address convergence when the consumer\nre-materializes. No test covers this refusal (step_test only pins the hash-mismatch arm at\ntest/dao/jing/remote/step_test.cljc:267-285). The fix report's claim \"All ingress sites\n(remote, ...) still call strict decode\" is false for this site. | After `accepted-bytes` hash\nverification, decode strictly at step.cljc:304 — replace `jing/segment-value` with\n`cbor/decode` (mirroring `dao.jing.remote/accept-bytes!` at remote.cljc:35-51) or add a\nstrict `jing` wrapper, mapping refusal onto the existing `::refused`/integrity-failure arm;\nadd a step_test case feeding a hash-valid noncanonical payload and asserting the\nintegrity-failure completion.\n\nP3 | src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:365 | The r2 copy-first restructure re-indented make-put's\ncollision arm; the message line is 81 columns (verified `length=81`). | Split the literal like\nthe mem backend does (mem.cljc:66-67), reflowed under the throw.\n\nP3 | (process) Dart host | The delta's ClojureDart branches (`debruijn.cljc:235` :cljd arm of\nthe changed classifier; carrier code in cbor.cljc) have no green-run evidence: the r2 fix\nreport discloses the Dart suite was still building, and the round-3 orchestrator evidence\ncovers JVM and Node only, while acceptance requires all three hosts\n(docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:568-569, 656). | Run the Dart suite on the final tree before\nmerge; static inspection of the :cljd branches shows parity, but it is the only unexercised\nhost for the changed code.\n\nRulings summary: r2 findings 1, 2, 4, 5, 6 CLOSED; finding 3 PARTIALLY CLOSED (trusted-read\nsplit correct, one ingress site regressed). The P1 is a regression the fix delta introduced in\nexactly the attack surface the brief flagged, so the delta cannot ship as-is.\n\nVerdict: REQUEST CHANGES\nI found another remote receipt path outside the stepped client: the blocking `content-client` turns a found Base64 reply into bytes and returns them to `jing/get`, whose trusted-snapshot decoder no longer checks canonicality. I’m checking its response contract and test coverage now; this appears to leave the same hash-valid, noncanonical payload exposure that the new stepped-client test closes.\nThe remaining gap is in the blocking RPC client, not the stepped client: it returns hash-valid response bytes without the required canonicality check. A forged address over noncanonical bytes can therefore reach `jing/get`’s snapshot decoder. I’m doing a final pass over the changed paths and line references before the verdict.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-24 19:23:41 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 02:23:41 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n**R2 finding 3: PARTIALLY CLOSED.** The stepped client now strictly decodes a hash-verified receipt and maps refusal to `integrity-failure` ([step.cljc:316](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/remote/step.cljc:316)). Its single opaque-object sentinel avoids both keyword identity differences and collision with a decoded value ([step.cljc:269](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/remote/step.cljc:269)). The new test uses an address hashed over noncanonical `18 01` bytes, so it exercises canonicality independently of the digest check ([step_test.cljc:275](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing/remote/step_test.cljc:275)).\n\nP1 | [remote.cljc:136](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc:136) | The blocking `content-client` returns a found reply after Base64 decoding alone ([remote.cljc:139](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc:139)). `jing/get` then uses `segment-value`, which hash-verifies but calls `decode-snapshot` rather than checking canonicality ([jing.cljc:457](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:457)). A hostile server can therefore serve hash-valid, noncanonical bytes under their own address and expose the decoded value, contrary to the remote-ingress contract ([dao.jing.cbor.md:124](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:124)). | Hash-verify and call strict `cbor/decode` on each found blocking-client receipt before returning its bytes; add the same hostile-pair test to `remote_test.cljc`.\n\nThe Dart list fix clears constructor metadata only on the newly built projected list ([debruijn.cljc:1006](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1006)); it does not discard arbitrary `:tag` metadata in the codec, and the Merkle value encoder does not use metadata ([debruijn.cljc:897](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:897), [dao.jing.cbor.md:179](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:179)). The quoted Dart fixtures address the constructor-metadata failure ([jing_test.cljc:225](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing_test.cljc:225), [vm_test.cljc:625](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm_test.cljc:625)). I did not rerun the suites; the orchestrator records all three lanes green ([orchestrator-log.md:6941](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/orchestrator-log.md:6941)).\n\nVerdict: REQUEST CHANGES\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-24 19:58:45 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 02:58:45 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n**R4 P1: CLOSED.** The blocking client now parses strict Base64, hash-verifies the requested address, and strictly decodes the payload before returning bytes ([remote.cljc:139](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc:139), [remote.cljc:157](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc:157)). Malformed found replies throw; absent replies return the caller’s not-found value without exposing bytes ([remote.cljc:164](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc:164)). A refusal also propagates through `jing/get` and the `materialize!` read-back before either can accept the reply ([jing.cljc:521](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:521), [jing.cljc:560](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:560)). The hostile-pair test targets the repaired boundary ([remote_test.cljc:407](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing/remote_test.cljc:407)).\n\nThe linker reconciliation is consistent with its documented fail-closed catch: a rejected RPC receipt becomes `:absent`, while local corrupt bytes reach the linker’s own `:address-mismatch` check ([debruijn_linker.cljc:182](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linker.cljc:182), [debruijn_linker.cljc:249](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linker.cljc:249), [debruijn_linker_test.cljc:233](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/debruijn_linker_test.cljc:233)). The new lines checked are ASCII and at most 80 columns. I did not rerun suites.\n\nP3 | [debruijn_linker_test.cljc:252](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/debruijn_linker_test.cljc:252) | The test name still says `...is-an-address-mismatch`, but its assertion now requires `:absent` ([debruijn_linker_test.cljc:267](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/debruijn_linker_test.cljc:267)). | Rename it to reflect the asserted classification.\n\nVerdict: READY\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-24 20:00:20 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 03:00:20 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\nThe r5 P3 is closed. The test is now named `corrupt-rpc-response-is-classified-absent`, matching its `:absent` assertion, and it is the only occurrence of that name in source and tests ([debruijn_linker_test.cljc:252](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/debruijn_linker_test.cljc:252), [debruijn_linker_test.cljc:267](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/debruijn_linker_test.cljc:267)). No actionable findings. I did not rerun tests.\n\nVerdict: READY\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
