{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git show --stat --oneline cab33f84; nl -ba docs/agents/roles/vm-engineer.md | sed -n '1,31p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md | sed -n '1,9p;122,137p;147,162p;179,187p;519,532p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md | sed -n '1,5p;285,307p;508,539p;652,668p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.md | sed -n '535,543p'\"","aggregated_output":"cab33f84 docs(design): clear the CBOR rebuild-readiness gate\n docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md | 20 ++++++++++++++++++--\n 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)\n     1\t---\n     2\tdescription: Yin.VM & Continuation Runtime Engineer role definition for datom.world\n     3\t---\n     4\t\n     5\t# ROLE: Yin.VM Runtime Engineer\n     6\t\n     7\t## Domain Scope\n     8\t\n     9\t- CESK semantics, evaluator implementations, and execution parity\n    10\t- Serializable continuations, suspension, transport, and resumption\n    11\t- Instruction dispatch, frames, environments, stores, and continuations\n    12\t- Runtime and compile-time macro boundaries\n    13\t- Effect scheduling, stream integration, REPL execution, and telemetry\n    14\t\n    15\tThis role owns no permanent file list. Each task defines the artifacts it may\n    16\tinspect or change and any permitted expansion.\n    17\t\n    18\t## Core Responsibilities\n    19\t\n    20\t1. **CESK Machine Integrity**: Maintain formal correspondence between Control, Environment, Store, and Kontinuation states.\n    21\t2. **Continuation Portability**: Ensure computations serialize as portable datoms that can pause, travel across DaoStream boundaries, and resume seamlessly.\n    22\t3. **Execution Parity**: Guarantee that semantic interpreter, AST walker, register VM, and stack VM produce identical output across identical inputs.\n    23\t4. **Performance & Benchmarking**: Optimize VM dispatch loops and register frames without violating functional purity.\n    24\t\n    25\t## Implementation Prompt Template\n    26\t\n    27\t```text\n    28\tCreated-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\n    29\tCreated-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\n    30\t\n    31\t# Task: <Task Name>\n     1\t# DaoJing: Backend-Independent CBOR Storage\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: implementation plan; not yet implemented. Architecture-reviewed\n     4\t2026-09-17 (`collab/1789680000000-architect-review-dao-jing-cbor.*`): no\n     5\tblocking findings, one medium-severity ambiguity (file-backend frame\n     6\townership, corrected below), and one cross-cutting change (the\n     7\t`dao.space.index`/`query` comparator changes) flagged as needing explicit\n     8\tsign-off from `dao.space`'s owners before this plan is built.\n     9\t\n   122\t  exactly when bytes arrive from an untrusted party, such as a remote/DHT\n   123\t  server checking a client's claim.\n   124\t- Verify every retrieved payload against its requested address before\n   125\t  decoding exactly one supported value. Canonicality verification (decode,\n   126\t  re-encode, byte-compare) occurs exactly once at each ingress of bytes Jing\n   127\t  did not encode itself: remote/DHT receipt and file replay acceptance,\n   128\t  before storage, caching, or exposure. Ordinary reads of accepted snapshots\n   129\t  hash-verify and decode without re-encoding. Validation belongs to the\n   130\t  shared codec/wrapper at those boundaries, not to backend value handlers;\n   131\t  immutable local forwarding does not repeat ingress validation. Missing\n   132\t  content remains distinct from stored nil, which has a CBOR representation.\n   133\t- Store immutable snapshots. Memory backends copy byte arrays on insertion\n   134\t  and retrieval so caller mutation cannot change previously addressed\n   135\t  content. Other backends must provide the same observable isolation.\n   136\t- Keep backend handles and composition explicit. Introduce no global\n   137\t  backend registry, ambient codec options, or domain-specific interpretation.\n   147\tSupported values are nil, booleans, strings, keywords, symbols, byte strings,\n   148\tvectors, finite lists/sequences, maps, sets, metadata, integers,\n   149\tfloating-point numbers, big integers, decimals, and ratios. Reject arbitrary\n   150\trecords, functions, and unsupported host objects before storage. The numeric\n   151\tcarriers described below are explicitly supported exceptions to the record\n   152\trestriction; arbitrary Boring records do not become Jing values. **This is\n   153\ta narrower domain than today's transitional `pr-str`-based encoder, which\n   154\taddresses anything printable/readable** — characters, `#inst`, `#uuid`, and\n   155\tother tagged literals are addressable today and have no slot in this\n   156\tsupported-values list or in Jing's four named CBOR extensions; they become\n   157\trejected, unsupported values once this plan lands. No current producer\n   158\temits them, so nothing observed breaks, but \"existing stores... must be\n   159\trebuilt together\" (*Addressing and clean break*) presumes every stored\n   160\tvalue is re-encodable under the new domain — an assumption this delta\n   161\tshould be checked against before rebuild, not discovered during it.\n   162\t\n   179\t- Preserve collection and symbol metadata using Boring's `clojure/with-meta`\n   180\t  mapping. Strip `:line`, `:column`, `:end-line`, and `:end-column`; omit empty\n   181\t  metadata. Clear metadata on internally constructed Dart collections\n   182\t  before attaching supplied metadata. Do not strip arbitrary user `:tag`\n   183\t  metadata as a workaround for Dart constructor metadata. This clearing\n   184\t  covers only collections Jing constructs at decode: a Dart producer\n   185\t  building a payload with `list` must still clear ClojureDart's constructor\n   186\t  metadata itself (`(with-meta (apply list xs) nil)`); that `dao.jing.md`\n   187\t  open item carries forward, it is not retired by this plan.\n   519\t**Owner ruling (2026-09-22): rebuild-readiness gate cleared.** This is a\n   520\tdevelopment repository with no deployed store, no published index\n   521\tmanifest, no externally held Jing address, and no retained intake history\n   522\toutside it. The orchestrator's repository-side audit found no committed\n   523\tproduction store or manifest (only unrelated build artifacts), no\n   524\tcharacter/`#inst`/`#uuid` literal reaching a Jing-addressed value (the two\n   525\tcandidates, `random-uuid` and `js/Date.now` in `yin.vm.telemetry` and\n   526\t`yin.repl`, are already stringified or numeric before use), and confirmed\n   527\t`test/dao/data/psset_fixtures.cljc` as the one committed old-address\n   528\tfixture already flagged for regeneration in step 5. With nothing deployed,\n   529\tthere is no unrecoverable content at risk and no rebuild to perform: the\n   530\tclean break authorized by this document has no remaining precondition.\n   531\tBoth owner gates on step 3 (this one and the `dao.space` comparator gate\n   532\tabove) are now cleared.\n     1\t# Multihash-Style Content Addressing for DaoJing and yin.vm\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design, architect signed off; not implemented\n     4\t\n     5\t## Decision\n   285\tThe signature remains:\n   286\t\n   287\t```clojure\n   288\t(get handle address not-found)\n   289\t```\n   290\t\n   291\tIt accepts every address recognized by `segment-address?`, including both initial algorithms. It rejects arbitrary keys before consulting the backend.\n   292\t\n   293\t`get` need not rehash every successful read. Existing backend insertion and replay checks, plus higher-level verified readers, retain their responsibilities. A consumer that verifies fetched content uses `segment-matches?`.\n   294\t\n   295\t### File-backend codec round-trip\n   296\t\n   297\t`dao.jing.file/validate-codec-round-trip!` is not an address/payload validator. It compares a payload's identity with its own `pr-str`/EDN round trip to ensure that the file codec does not discard address-significant information.\n   298\t\n   299\tIt does not use `segment-matches?`. Both sides must be hashed with the algorithm selected for the pending address:\n   300\t\n   301\t```clojure\n   302\t(content-hash payload  {:algorithm algorithm})\n   303\t(content-hash replayed {:algorithm algorithm})\n   304\t```\n   305\t\n   306\tThis makes explicit SHA-256 materialization and default BLAKE3 materialization obey the same file-codec rule.\n   307\t\n   508\t## Sequencing with canonical CBOR\n   509\t\n   510\tThe algorithm-registry epic landed first, over the order-normalized print\n   511\tencoder of the time; the canonical CBOR clean break then replaced that\n   512\tencoder on 2026-09-24. Both are complete.\n   513\t\n   514\tThe registry epic established:\n   515\t\n   516\t- the flat multihash address grammar;\n   517\t- permanent BLAKE3 and SHA-256 entries;\n   518\t- BLAKE3 as the minting default;\n   519\t- explicit SHA-256 minting;\n   520\t- algorithm-directed verification; and\n   521\t- algorithm-preserving copy paths.\n   522\t\n   523\tCanonical CBOR was a separate clean-break epic, now landed. The codec in\n   524\t`dao.jing.cbor`, whose byte contract is frozen by\n   525\t`test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json`, is the addressing encoder:\n   526\t`canonical-bytes` is `dao.jing.cbor/encode`. The algorithm registry was\n   527\tunchanged by that landing:\n   528\t\n   529\t- BLAKE3 remains the default;\n   530\t- SHA-256 remains explicitly selectable;\n   531\t- the address grammar remains `:segment/<algorithm-id>-<digest>`;\n   532\t- every digest and every DaoJing address was regenerated from CBOR bytes\n   533\t  (`digest-table.edn`'s canonical rows, the psset fixtures, and every\n   534\t  pinned test hash);\n   535\t- development stores, fixtures, manifests, ASTs, and continuations were\n   536\t  rebuilt together; and\n   537\t- no printer-address reader, alias, graph migration, or encoding\n   538\t  negotiation was introduced.\n   539\t\n   652\t\n   653\t## Explicit non-goals\n   654\t\n   655\tThis plan does not:\n   656\t\n   657\t- encode a canonical-format identifier in segment addresses;\n   658\t- support simultaneous canonical encoders;\n   659\t- preserve segment addresses across a canonical-encoding change;\n   660\t- build a migration path for pre-registry stores or addresses;\n   661\t- add an old-address alias or dual checkpoint reader;\n   662\t- distinguish SHA-256 content by age or provenance;\n   663\t- deprecate SHA-256;\n   664\t- force callers to use BLAKE3 when they explicitly choose SHA-256;\n   665\t- adopt IPFS binary multihash or CID serialization;\n   666\t- introduce runtime algorithm registration;\n   667\t- negotiate algorithms implicitly between peers;\n   668\t- implement or activate canonical CBOR addressing;\n   535\t- **Decoded numeric carriers on JavaScript.** Floating-point, decimal, and\n   536\t  rational content decode to Jing's portable carriers on JavaScript, never\n   537\t  to a bare number; a consumer that reads values back from a store and\n   538\t  pattern-matches on host number types (today, `yin.vm.debruijn`'s slot\n   539\t  type check) must accept the carriers, per `dao.jing.cbor.md`'s numeric\n   540\t  identity section.\n   541\t- **Durable observer checkpoints / long-running runner.** `observer-state`\n   542\t  and `observe-step!` are single-step and in-process. The checkpoint records,\n   543\t  per member, the stream coordinate plus the transport-minted cursor; the\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-24 20:20:38 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 03:20:38 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n**No blocking architectural defect found.** The following documentation corrections are nonblocking:\n\nP2 | [dao.jing.hash-registry.md:293](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:293) | Its still-normative text says `get` need not hash-verify reads and describes a `pr-str`/EDN file round trip at [line 297](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:297). The current contract requires hash verification on reads and strict CBOR validation at file replay ([dao.jing.cbor.md:124](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:124), [file.cljc:118](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:118)). | Mark those registry-era sections historical or replace them with the current read and replay rules.\n\nP3 | [dao.jing.cbor.md:147](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:147) | “Supported” lists are not unqualified on Dart: a runtime-created list can carry an unsupported constructor `:tag`, and the VM’s reader-position projection can reattach it ([vm.cljc:854](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:854), [vm.cljc:858](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:858)). The warning at [line 179](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:179) names Dart producers but should explicitly include internal producers. | Qualify the supported-value wording and broaden the producer warning. Keep the VM correction with the VM Runtime owner, not in the codec.\n\nP3 | [dao.jing.cbor.md:3](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:3) | The header still calls the swap “not yet implemented”; the registry document does likewise despite recording both epics complete ([dao.jing.hash-registry.md:3](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:3), [dao.jing.hash-registry.md:508](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:508)). | Update the status headers after landing.\n\nThe swap introduces no ambient codec registry or new shared mutable state: the algorithm registry is immutable, memory state stays behind copying closures, and the file backend owns only framing, not value interpretation ([jing.cljc:213](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:213), [mem.cljc:108](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:108), [file.cljc:118](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:118)). It adds no graph assumptions or semantic interpretation to Jing ([dao.jing.md:288](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.md:288)). The synchronous byte-store functions are the explicitly retained handle boundary, with an effect-stream write path deferred; the swap does not add an upward application callback ([dao.jing.cbor.md:98](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:98), [dao.jing.md:549](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.md:549)).\n\nThe ingress split is coherent: canonical bytes are minted once, remote and DHT receipts strictly validate, file replay validates before mutation, and accepted-snapshot reads hash-verify without re-encoding ([jing.cljc:513](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:513), [remote.cljc:157](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc:157), [dht.cljc:167](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:167), [file.cljc:281](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:281), [jing.cljc:457](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:457)). The private stepped-client host-object sentinel cannot escape as decoded content ([step.cljc:269](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/remote/step.cljc:269)). Dart constructor metadata is cleared at the corrected de Bruijn projection, while user `:tag` metadata remains subject to the codec contract ([debruijn.cljc:1006](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1006), [cbor.cljc:1069](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:1069)).\n\nThe `vm.cljc` residual is a loud, scoped producer limitation, recorded for the VM owner, not a reason to weaken metadata preservation or block this swap ([orchestrator-log.md:7011](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/orchestrator-log.md:7011)). The clean break remains authorized by the owner’s dev-repository rebuild ruling; I found no production legacy decoder or address alias ([dao.jing.cbor.md:506](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:506), [dao.jing.cbor.md:519](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:519)). I did not rerun suites; the orchestrator records green Dart, JVM, and Node lanes on the final tree ([orchestrator-log.md:6998](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/orchestrator-log.md:6998)).\n\nSign-off: GRANTED"}}
